            "models": [
                {
                    "id": "workforce",
                    "label": { "en": "Workforce" },
                    "description": {
                        "en": "A workforce is any group of people engaged in or available for work. Examples include the members of a team, the employees in an organization or division, and the labour force within a country or region.\n\nWorkforce data documents the relationship between [Organisations](/contentModels/entities/organisationEntity.md) (employers) and [People](/contentModels/entities/personEntity.md) (workers). For a more detailed explanation of organisations and people please refer to the [Party](/contentModels/party/partyIndex.md) content model.\n\nOrganisations that employ or contract workers will commonly define [Jobs](/contentModels/entities/jobEntity.md) to identify the type of work that is performed, and [Positions](/contentModels/entities/positionEntity.md) to identify individual instances of those jobs. Managing the organisations establishment (jobs & positions) is an important activity that can link directly with the [Recruitment & Onboarding](/contentModels/humanResource/recruitmentAndOnboardingModel.md) function.\n\nThe people who make up the active workforce are most commonly referred to as employees or contractors. However, workforce records may also include inactive people such as employee dependants or pensioners.\n\nThe relationship between an organisation and a person is termed an [Employment](/contentModels/entities/employmentEntity.md). It can also be referred to as and engagement, term of employment or work contract. Over time an organisation can contract or employ many people, and a person can, be employed by, or work for many organisations. At the same time an organisation will commonly have many workers and a person may work for multiple organisations.\n\nWithin a period of employment a person may occupy one or more positions. Each [Occupancy](/contentModels/entities/occupancyEntity.md) represents the relationship between an employment and a position. Depending on the work practices of the organisation, a person may occupy multiple positions at the same time, effectively splitting their time at work across different jobs or functions."
                    },
                    "firstCreatedAt": null,
                    "icon": null,
                    "iconDark": null,
